  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 405 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nOn Monday, Upper-level forecast guidance is in good agreement\nfor zonal flow to occur over the area that transitions into\nsouthwesterly flow midday as a trough forms over the Four\nCorners region. Then on Tuesday, the upper-level pattern is then\nexpected to switch from southwesterly to northwesterly as the\ntrough moves over the area and amplifies to the southeast into\nnorthern Oklahoma while a ridge forms over the middle Rockies \nand quickly amplifies to the north. with this being said there \nis some slight disagreement with where the trough could be in \nthe morning as it begins to amplify. Some ensemble members are \nhinting that the 500 mb 564 height trough could be further\nsouthwest into New Mexico whereas a smaller handful are hinting\nthat this could be in northern Texas. There is medium to high\nconfidence (45-65%) that the trough will remain in southwest\nkansas. \n\nOvernight Monday and Tuesday morning, we expect some snowfall to\noccur over the County Warning Area (CWA) as the trough passes\nthrough and mixes with deep layer moisture advecting in from the\nsouth. While there is a 50% chance that the vast majority of the\nCWA will see a trace of snowfall, ensembles are hinting at two\nmain scenarios that could show higher amount of snowfall towards\nthe eastern or southern portions of the CWA. The first scenario,\nthe NBM and GEFS are showing that 1 to 3 inches of snow could\nfall between the KS-25 and US-83 corridor as weak stability and\na column of slightly higher moisture over the area could be\npresent as the trough passes. The second scenario is a higher\namount of snow occurring along and south of I-70 between\nBurlington and Colby as well as between US-287 and US-83 if the\noverall trough pattern does move further south. This would mix\nwith even more moisture and could allow up to 3 inches of snow.\nEither way, minor driving impacts on I-70 and US-40 are\npossible. Depending on if snowfall occurs, confidence for the\nfirst scenario is 60% while confidence for the second scenario\nis even lower at 30% to occur, there is a 10% that less than\nhalf an inch of snowfall is the maximum amount of snowfall for\nthis event. \n\nOn Wednesday, northwest flow is forecast as the ridge moves\neastward over the Rockies into eastern Montana. On Thursday and\nFriday, forecast confidence decreases as multiple troughs form\nover the western CONUS. GEFS mean spread is favoring for\nnorthwesterly flow with a weak trough briefly passing through\nFriday morning. \n\nTemperatures for next week look to follow the similar pattern\nfrom yesterday's forecast, but with a slightly warmer trend\nafter the system passes through on Tuesday. Highs are forecast\nto be in the mid 30s to mid 40s on Monday and Tuesday, before\nwarming back up into the upper 40s for the remainder of the\nperiod. Morning lows will likely stay in the 20s.",
